B2B lead generation for IT services works best when you can name the project a buyer may need help with. A retail CTO evaluating integrations has a different problem from a logistics operations lead replacing a manual workflow. Both might buy software development, but each needs a different reason to take a meeting. Pick a project your team knows well, find companies where the need is plausible, and use the first conversation to test your assumptions.

## Choose a project you can explain in one sentence

Start by reviewing your completed work. Look for a pattern in the problem the client had, the systems involved, the buyer who approved the work and the outcome the team could observe. “Custom software development” leaves all of that for the buyer to infer. “Integrating warehouse and order systems after a retailer adds another fulfillment location” gives the buyer a concrete situation to recognize.

Write down what your team can own and where it needs help from the client. If the work requires access to a legacy system, a technical contact or a procurement review, include those constraints in your qualification notes. Then choose a segment where you have evidence to share. One coherent offer for a clearly defined buyer makes it easier to learn from replies.

## Build an account list around buying signals

Company size, industry and region tell you whether an account could fit. A [buying signal](/resources/glossary/buying-signal) gives you a reason to look more closely now. A job description for integration engineers, a new fulfillment site or a public migration announcement can each point to a potential project. Keep the source and date alongside the observation so someone else on your team can check it.

For example, imagine a mid-sized retailer announces another warehouse and advertises for an engineer to work on inventory integrations. That supports a hypothesis about adding another inventory feed. Ask whether the new location changes how the retailer synchronizes stock. Record any evidence that weakens the hypothesis too, such as an announcement that an existing vendor already owns the rollout. Use an [account targeting strategy](/resources/blogs/account-targeting-strategy) to separate companies worth deeper research from those you should revisit later.

## Reach the people who own the work

Map the [buying committee](/resources/glossary/buying-committee) before you write. An engineering lead may care about the systems a partner would touch and who maintains them after delivery. An operations leader may care about stock accuracy and manual reconciliation. A CTO or procurement lead may need to understand vendor risk and the approval process. Use the same project hypothesis with a question that fits each person’s responsibility.

Start with the person closest to the work. If they point you to someone else, carry that context forward. A short email might say, “I saw you’re adding a fulfillment site. Will the new warehouse share inventory with the existing order system from day one?” A useful follow-up can explain how you would test inventory reconciliation before a rollout. Coordinate email and [LinkedIn outreach](/services/linkedin-outreach) around the conversation so colleagues at one account receive consistent information.

## Show what you delivered and qualify the next step

Choose proof that matches the buyer’s project. Explain the client’s situation, the work your team owned and the observable result. A small example with a clear connection to the buyer’s situation gives them something to evaluate. If a client name or metric is confidential, use an approved anonymized example and describe the work you can substantiate.

Myntmore’s [US software development case study](/case-studies/agencies-it-usa-software-development) shows what this looks like in practice. The client was an Indian software development firm targeting mid-sized US retail and logistics companies. The campaign grouped accounts by relevant capabilities and business context, then qualified interested buyers by scope, timing and decision responsibility. Myntmore reports 24 meetings booked, 19 held, seven companies sharing defined requirements and four requesting proposals. Those figures describe that engagement. Track the same stages in your own campaign so you can tell whether meetings are producing work your team can scope.

On a discovery call, ask what triggered the conversation, who owns the project and which decision comes next. Clarify whether the buyer wants a scoped project, help from a specialist or a long-term delivery partner. If there is a fit, agree on a concrete next step, such as reviewing an integration map with the technical owner.

## Run a focused 30-day pilot

In week one, pick one offer and agree on what a qualified conversation looks like. Build a small account list and collect one credible observation per company. In week two, check contacts, match each segment to relevant proof and draft the outreach. Give reply handling to someone who can ask follow-up questions and recognize a poor fit.

In weeks three and four, review replies alongside the account evidence. If interested prospects keep raising a different problem, revisit your assumptions. If the right people reply but rarely take meetings, review the question you ask and the proof you show. Track meetings held, requirements shared and opportunities to scope work. At the end of the month, use those records to decide which segment deserves another round.
